{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1132","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1132","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1132\",\"parent_revision\":\"ef247267a5c9f21c69bd3db3bb90082045d182fd\",\"root_revision\":\"a8c7fbad0bd7bd440bb1f9dddba770bd764096b3b13a6dd8f7c878622999f798\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen backlog_pull or backlog_sync fetches GitHub issue state and finds an issue is closed but the local workflow status is still open/in-progress/needs-grooming, the item enters an inconsistent state. Today this is silently ignored \u2014 the local file stays stale (observed: #986 showed as open in backlog_list despite being closed and verified on GitHub).\\n\\nWhat should happen:\\n\\n1. During pull/sync, when an issue's GitHub state is \\\"closed\\\" but local status is not terminal (not \\\"done\\\", \\\"resolved\\\", \\\"closed\\\"), flag the item as needing reconciliation\\n2. Add the item to a verification queue \u2014 a list of items where GitHub says closed but local workflow never completed\\n3. A local agent (or the next groom/work invocation) works through the queue: for each item, check acceptance criteria against the codebase (search for merged PRs, check if the work was done, verify ACs pass)\\n4. If ACs pass \u2192 call backlog_resolve with evidence (PR numbers, commit SHAs)\\n5. If ACs do not pass \u2192 reopen the GitHub issue with a comment explaining what is missing, update local status back to in-progress\\n\\nThe reconciliation observed during this session (\\\"Reconciled #1046, #1071, #1078, #1077, #1076, #1063 \u2014 updated local status to closed\\\") only updates the local status field. It does not verify whether the work was actually completed. Closing the local status without checking ACs is the same problem as closing an issue without a PR \u2014 it loses the audit trail.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **backlog_pull/sync must reconcile closed-but-unresolved items into a verification queue** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 #986 appeared in open backlog_list results despite being closed/verified on GitHub. Reconciliation during pull updated 6 items but only changed status, did not verify ACs.\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-30\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-02)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 Silent data loss: items get stamped `closed` without AC verification, losing the audit trail. P1 is correct \u2014 this is a data integrity issue in a core workflow tool used every session.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: any workflow that relies on `backlog_list` showing accurate open/in-progress items (the #986 incident showed a verified item still appearing as open)\\n- Bottleneck: reconciliation runs on every `backlog_pull` / `backlog_sync` call; the current silent stamp means downstream agents act on stale status\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Items closed on GitHub without a PR or AC verification will surface for review instead of silently disappearing\\n- `backlog_resolve` will be called with evidence (PR numbers, commit SHAs), creating an audit trail\\n- Items closed prematurely on GitHub can be reopened automatically, keeping the workflow honest\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `backlog_pull` or `backlog_sync` detects that a GitHub issue is closed but the local status is not terminal (not `done`, `resolved`, or `closed`), the item is added to a verification queue rather than immediately stamped `closed`. The queue is a persistent file listing items that need AC verification. A separate drain command works through the queue: for each item it checks whether the work was actually done (merged PRs, passing ACs), then calls `backlog_resolve` with evidence or reopens the GitHub issue.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n- A verification queue file at `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/verification-queue.json` containing a list of `{issue_number, title, file_path, closed_at, local_status_at_detection}` records\\n- `_reconcile_single_closed_issue` and `_reconcile_closed_issues` append to the queue instead of immediately stamping `closed`\\n- A new `backlog_drain_queue` MCP tool (exposed in `server.py`) reads the queue, calls the AC-checking logic per item, and calls `backlog_resolve` or reopens the issue\\n- The reconcile functions return a count of items queued (in addition to the existing reconciled count) so the caller can report \\\"N items added to verification queue\\\"\\n- The `backlog_sync` / `backlog_pull` response dict gains a `queued` key alongside `refreshed` and `reconciled`\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Running `backlog_sync` when a GitHub issue is closed but local status is `in-progress` does NOT set local status to `closed`; instead the item appears in `verification-queue.json`\\n2. Running `backlog_sync` a second time for the same item does not add a duplicate entry to the queue\\n3. The `backlog_sync` response dict contains a `queued` key with the count of newly queued items\\n4. Running `backlog_drain_queue` with an item whose merged PR satisfies ACs results in `backlog_resolve` being called and the item removed from the queue\\n5. Running `backlog_drain_queue` with an item whose ACs are not satisfied results in the GitHub issue being reopened and the item removed from the queue\\n6. `backlog_list` (without `include_closed=True`) does not show items that are in the verification queue (they remain at their pre-queue local status until drained)\\n7. `uv run pytest` passes after the change\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py \u2014 `_reconcile_single_closed_issue` (line 1237), `_reconcile_closed_issues` (line 1267), `_sync_incremental` (line 1324), `_sync_full` (line 1363), `pull_items` (line 2747) |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py \u2014 `backlog_sync` (line 522), `backlog_pull` (line 841) |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py \u2014 `BacklogItem` (line 421), `_TERMINAL_STATUSES` (line 1518) |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/frontmatter_utils.py \u2014 `update_item_metadata` used by reconcile functions |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None identified\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Verification queue persistence schema not yet defined (location and JSON schema need agreement before implementation)\\n- Drain command AC-checking logic is complex: checking merged PRs and verifying ACs requires GitHub API calls and codebase search \u2014 scope may need to be bounded (e.g., first version just flags items for human review rather than auto-resolving)\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 the reconcile path change is localized to 3\u20134 functions in `operations.py` and 2 MCP tool registrations in `server.py`. The drain command AC-checking logic is the uncertain part; a first version that surfaces items for human review (without auto-resolve) is Small.\\n\",\"digest\":\"589adb7cdcac93a91f8a986926f962297e66de95607cc039d7c9e20e9eb90add\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnIBRA\"}"}